WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.700
You spend weeks carefully assembling a massive intricate project.

2
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.400
You put the very last piece in place,

3
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.900
step back, and watch as the entire thing shatters to pieces

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.400
because of one bad block buried at the very bottom.

5
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.700
Building a mobile app with standard code

6
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.300
is exactly like free building from a massive bin of those plastic blocks.

7
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.700
Complete freedom sounds great, but it's risky.

8
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.500
You can accidentally grab a fragile or incorrect piece of code,

9
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.000
stick it right into your foundation, and nothing stops you.

10
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.800
There is no immediate warming.

11
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.900
That structural mistake stays hidden while you're building.

12
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.100
You only discover it weeks later,

13
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.800
as a massive app crash.

14
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.100
The moment a real user taps a button on their phone.

15
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:48.200
When there are no rules to check the work as it's being done,

16
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.900
tiny weaknesses eventually lead to a total collapse.

17
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.500
But what if those building blocks were smart?

18
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.800
What if you tried to snap two pieces together that didn't fit

19
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.000
and they actually pushed back refusing to connect?

20
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.500
For mobile app developers, a tool called TypeScript acts like these smart blocks.

21
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.900
It requires you to define the type,

22
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.400
the precise shape and intended purpose

23
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.400
of every single piece of code before you ever start building.

24
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.000
This system acts as an active, magical blueprint.

25
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:21.000
It constantly checks your work.

26
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.900
If you try to force pieces together that don't match,

27
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.100
it stops you right then and there.

28
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.700
The real power here is that it finds mistakes

29
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.400
while the app is still on the developer's desk,

30
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.300
long before it ever reaches a user's phone.

31
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.200
Let's look at how this works on a basic part of an app

32
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.500
like a standard button you might tap to submit a form.

33
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.400
To show up correctly, this button needs specific instructions.

34
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.100
For example, it needs a valid color code

35
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.000
so the app knows what its background should look like.

36
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.700
In this diagram, a developer is making a mistake

37
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.600
by accidentally telling the button its color is the word Tuesday.

38
00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.000
Because Tuesday is a time and not a color,

39
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.300
the system recognizes the mismatch immediately.

40
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.400
The bad instruction bounces right off

41
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.300
and the app won't move forward until fixed.

42
00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.600
This ensures that the visual parts of the app

43
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.500
follow a strict logic,

44
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.200
preventing weird graphical glitches from reaching the user.

45
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.000
This same logic applies to how we move between screens.

46
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.100
A single mobile app might have dozens of different screens

47
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.900
that all need to link together perfectly.

48
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.400
To keep things organized,

49
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.500
developers create a master map

50
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.900
that lists every single destination

51
00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:36.300
and exactly what kind of information each screen expects to receive.

52
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.700
If we link a button to the setting screen,

53
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.600
but make a typo, spelling it with 1T,

54
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.900
the system checks the master map.

55
00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.100
It sees a misspelled setting screen doesn't exist

56
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.100
and the connection snaps back.

57
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.300
It forces you to point to a valid destination.

58
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.000
This eliminates dead ends,

59
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.000
making sure that every button press

60
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:58.300
actually leads where it's supposed to go.

61
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.000
Now, think about how an app gets information

62
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.400
from the outside world,

63
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.500
like downloading a profile picture from the internet.

64
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.600
This is always uncertain.

65
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:09.300
The internet might work perfectly,

66
00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.600
or the connection could drop the second the user enters a tunnel.

67
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.600
To handle this,

68
00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.000
the blueprint uses a rule

69
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.000
that forces the developer to account

70
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.600
for every possible outcome

71
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.900
before the app is even finished.

72
00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:21.900
In this diagram,

73
00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.000
the path from the cloud splits.

74
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.700
The developer must provide instructions

75
00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.000
for the success path,

76
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.100
but also a path for an error.

77
00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.300
If they ignore the error,

78
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.100
the system won't accept the code.

79
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:33.200
It stops everything

80
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.600
until they explain what happens

81
00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:36.200
if the internet fails.

82
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.100
This forces the app to be prepared

83
00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.000
for the messiness of the real world,

84
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.400
preventing it from freezing up

85
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.200
when things don't go perfectly.

86
00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.300
All of this measuring and rule following

87
00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.800
takes a lot of extra effort

88
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.700
from the developer right at the start.

89
00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:49.900
It's a trade-off.

90
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.600
You spend time meticulously defining

91
00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.200
every piece on the blueprint

92
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.900
before you get the satisfaction

93
00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:57.700
of seeing it all come together,

94
00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:01.100
but the payoff is a much higher level of stability.

95
00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:04.000
When the code is finally turned into a working app,

96
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.700
the most common structural mistakes

97
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.200
have already been caught and cleared away.

98
00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.800
For the person using the app,

99
00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.500
everything just works.

100
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.200
They see smooth transitions

101
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.000
and helpful error messages,

102
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.600
never realizing how many potential crashes

103
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.100
were prevented by the rules

104
00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.000
operating under the surface.

105
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.800
TypeScript doesn't change what an app can do.

106
00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.100
It just changes how well it's built,

107
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.000
turning a fragile collection of parts

108
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.200
into a solid, reliable structure.

